digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56722-z
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates whether mutant huntingtin accelerates epigenetic aging in striatal neurons by inducing PRC1 paralog switching that de-represses developmental genes and erodes neuronal identity, as a candidate explanation for selective vulnerability in Huntington's disease.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a disease-causing mutation selectively affects a neuronal population and multiple cell-type-resolved epigenomic observations implicate aging-like identity erosion and a chromatin-regulatory mechanism, a study can propose and evaluate that mechanism as an explanation for selective neuronal vulnerability."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) If HD striatal-neuron data show accelerated loss of epigenetic information, this supports interpreting the HD mutation as driving accelerated epigenetic aging in those neurons."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Altered PRC1 regulation together with accelerated developmental-gene de-repression in HD striatal neurons makes PRC1 perturbation, including PRC1-CBX paralog switching, a plausible linked mechanism rather than a proven sole cause."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) In Huntington's disease, PRC1-mediated accelerated epigenetic aging in striatal neurons can be treated as a candidate mechanism contributing to striatal-neuron vulnerability."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) In Huntington's disease, the combination of a toxic mutant huntingtin cause and selective striatal degeneration motivates focusing on molecular changes in vulnerable striatal neurons."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Direct and indirect spiny projection neurons in the striatum are selectively vulnerable in Huntington's disease."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) If epigenomic regulation maintains cell identity and epigenetic alteration is a hallmark of aging, then aging-related identity loss can plausibly be investigated as loss or remodeling of epigenetic information."];
  "n10" [label="(6,0,0) Currently the epigenome, consisting of chemical modifications on DNA and histones, regulates gene expression patterns and endows cells with specific identities and functions, but is susceptible to environmentally and damage-induced alterations over time."];
  "n17" [label="(8,0,0) Currently epigenomic analyses in bulk HD striatal tissue show early loss of H3K27ac at striatal super-enhancers, broad enhancers regulating cellular identity genes, which results in transcriptional downregulation of their targets, namely striatal identity genes."];
  "n18" [label="(8,1,16) Currently super-enhancers are broad enhancers that regulate genes defining tissue identity and neuronal function, and down-regulated genes in HD striatum often display decreased H3K27ac at such super-enhancers."];
  "n19" [label="(8,2,16) Currently the HD mutation globally accelerates age-dependent epigenetic and transcriptional reprogramming of brain cell identities, suggesting an age-like epigenetic remodeling in HD."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently there is evidence that HD is caused by an abnormal expansion of CAG trinucleotide repeats in the HTT gene, producing mutant huntingtin (mHTT) with a toxic polyglutamine expansion."];
  "n20" [label="(9,0,0) Currently earlier epigenomic analyses lacked cellular resolution and temporal resolution, so it remained unclear whether the specifically HD-vulnerable neurons themselves undergo accelerated epigenetic aging and accelerated de-repression of developmental genes."];
  "n21" [label="(0,0,0) If bulk-tissue epigenomic studies reveal age-like epigenetic changes, such as loss of H3K27ac at identity-gene super-enhancers, but lack cell-type and temporal resolution, then a plausible explanation for disease-related neuronal vulnerability is that vulnerable neurons may undergo accelerated epigenetic aging that is currently masked by averaging across cell types and time points."];
  "n22" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Given that bulk HD striatal tissue shows early loss of H3K27ac at super-enhancers and age-like epigenetic remodeling, and given that when such bulk changes are seen without cell-type and temporal resolution a plausible explanation is that particularly vulnerable neurons undergo accelerated epigenetic aging, therefore it is plausible that HD-vulnerable striatal neurons experience accelerated epigenetic aging and developmental gene de-repression, but this has not yet been directly demonstrated."];
  "n29" [label="(14,0,0) Currently the data show that the HD mutation accelerates loss of epigenetic information associated with cellular aging in striatal neurons of HD mice."];
  "n30" [label="(15,0,0) Currently the data show that epigenetic de-repression of developmental genes is dramatically accelerated in HD, and indicate that this accelerated de-repression involves PRC1 through paralog switching among PRC1-CBX genes."];
  "n32" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given that in this study’s multiple cell-type- and time-resolved datasets striatal neurons in HD mice repeatedly show accelerated loss of epigenetic information and developmental gene de-repression, and given that when such consistent patterns are observed it is justified to generalize that the HD mutation induces accelerated epigenetic aging of striatal neurons, therefore HD drives a neuron-intrinsic accelerated epigenetic aging program in striatal neurons."];
  "n33" [label="(13,1,0) Currently prior analyses and the present targeting strategy highlight that PRC1 subunits show altered regulation in HD striatal neurons, implying that PRC1 pathways are perturbed in these cells."];
  "n37" [label="(16,0,0) Currently the authors conclude that their data support the view that PRC1-mediated accelerated epigenetic aging is a key mechanism underlying striatal neuron vulnerability in HD."];
  "n4" [label="(3,0,0) Currently direct and indirect spiny projection neurons (dSPNs and iSPNs) in the striatum, which represent more than 95% of striatal neurons, are highly vulnerable to the HD mutation."];
  "n5" [label="(3,1,7) Currently in HD the brunt of the degenerative process involves the striatum, indicating selective vulnerability of striatal neurons."];
  "n9" [label="(5,0,0) Currently epigenetic alteration is recognized as a key hallmark of cellular aging."];
  "n17" -> "n22" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
